WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:08.940
Many conservatives saw the Brexit
referendum and Donald Trump's 2016

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.900
victory as useful correctives. It was
time to push back, the argument went,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.800
against the overreach of unaccountable
international bureaucracies, such as the

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.120
European Union and the UN. Maybe
even NATO has become obsolete.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.600
But can we really go back to an age of
sovereign nation-states? Not really.

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:30.440
For example, ten million US flags are imported into
the United States from China every year,

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:34.500
and a tightly integrated global economy
needs shared rules. Although reforms

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.190
of many international organizations are
needed, Western democracies cannot

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.340
disengage if they are to keep the
world's authoritarians at bay.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:47.820
Also, the age of sovereign nation-states is
mostly a myth. Throughout its history,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.880
Europe was marked by efforts to
reconcile diversity and unity through

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.700
federalist projects, such as the Holy
Roman Empire or the Hanseatic League.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:01.040
Those long predate the creation of the
modern nation-state in the late 19th century.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.200
Globalism is thus not a historic
aberration — nationalism is.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.440
